  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 AM EDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\n===== Dry and Sunny Over the Next Week =====\n\nAnother very clear morning across the eastern half of the US, with \nthe entire Ohio Valley under clear sky cover. It is chilly this \nmorning for most, with ridge-valley splits noted on the KY Mesonet, \nwith the cooler valleys dropping down into the 30s, but temps \nremaining in the 40s so far on the ridges. Expect temps to cool a \nbit more before sunrise given the winds have gone calm and boosting \nour radiational cooling. Patchy frost is expected, so the Frost \nAdvisory remains on track this morning.\n\nSfc high pressure will move directly through our area today, keeping \nthe skycover clear and winds light and variable. Temps should be \nable to climb into the upper 60s this afternoon, though some places \ntouching 70 will be possible too. \n\nExpect a rinse and repeat forecast for the rest of the week ahead, \nwith beautiful fall weather and an abundance of sunshine each day. \nThe upper pattern will feature a stagnant upper low over the East \nCoast, which will meander just off the Virgina/North Carolina coast \nfor several days as any upper level momentum remains north across \nCanada. Upstream of that feature, the upper levels will feature a \ngrowing dome of high pressure across the central US, which will \neventually become more amplified later in the week when a Pacific \nupper low comes ashore over the West Coast. With upper lows on \neither side of the US, the upper level ridge will amplify into a \nbroad omega block pattern during the second half of the week. \n\nAs a result of the amplified ridge pattern, we'll likely see temps \ngradually warm back up into the mid-70s by the weekend when the \nridge axis and warmer low-level return flow move over our area. Dry \nweather continues for the weekend, with no PoPs in the seven day \nforecast. \n\n===== Worsening Drought Conditions Possible ====\n\nWhile the beautiful fall weather will be nice to have, it does come \nwith a catch. This prolonged dry period could worsen drought \nconditions for our area. Almost our entire forecast area is now \nexperiencing at least abnormally dry (D0) conditions, with south-\ncentral KY even worse with moderate drought (D1). Since Sept 1, \nLouisville (SDF) and Lexington are both running more than 2.5\" below \nnormal rainfall for this time frame. Bowling Green is only behind \n0.58\" since Sept 1, but the bigger picture shows they are 5.25\" \nbelow normal rainfall since the start of 2026. With no rainfall \nexpected in the next seven days, these climatological departures \nfrom normal will only continue to spread.",
